{"items":[{"owner":{"account_id":1600,"reputation":2352,"user_id":2144,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":86,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/7bwnw.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Eric Pohl","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/2144/eric-pohl"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1221939042,"score":6,"last_activity_date":1219353548,"creation_date":1219353548,"answer_id":21217,"question_id":1711,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>In no particular order except how they're arranged on my bookshelf:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><em>The Pragmatic Programmer</em></li>\n<li><em>Rafactoring</em> by Fowler</li>\n<li><em>Working Effectively with Legacy Code</em> by Feathers. This is practically a companion volume to <em>Refactoring</em>.</li>\n<li><em>UML Distilled</em> by Fowler. Among its other virtues is brevity.</li>\n<li><em>Debugging the Development Process</em> by Steve Maguire</li>\n<li><em>Design Patterns</em> (aka \"Gang of Four\") by Gamma et al</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":1328,"reputation":7570,"user_id":1769,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":83,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/JpweY.png?s=256","display_name":"David Hayes","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1769/david-hayes"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1221939042,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1219353000,"creation_date":1219353000,"answer_id":21203,"question_id":1711,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>I have a couple of (rather old) blog posts on this subject</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.spindriftpages.net/blog/dave/2005/11/17/c-books/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://www.spindriftpages.net/blog/dave/2005/11/17/c-books/</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.spindriftpages.net/blog/dave/2005/06/06/good-oo-books/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://www.spindriftpages.net/blog/dave/2005/06/06/good-oo-books/</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.spindriftpages.net/blog/dave/2005/05/11/really-great-it-books/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://www.spindriftpages.net/blog/dave/2005/05/11/really-great-it-books/</a></li>\n<li>Although a good book I found Code\nComplete to be rather a dull read (a\ncontroversial view I admit).</li>\n<li>I like\nJeffery Richter and the books Joel\nSpolksy has written</li>\n<li>The Eric Meyer CSS books are really good too</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":518,"reputation":59263,"user_id":672,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":86,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/bZTmBR2U.png?s=256","display_name":"JosephStyons","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/672/josephstyons"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1221939042,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1219352094,"creation_date":1219352094,"answer_id":21161,"question_id":1711,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"https://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/com/1593271190\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Code Craft</a></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":853,"reputation":29775,"user_id":1116,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":69,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/d71e6f29b4a15b0a2038f28ee913a293?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Johnno Nolan","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1116/johnno-nolan"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1221939042,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1219349410,"creation_date":1219349410,"answer_id":21033,"question_id":1711,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"https://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/com/0201485672\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Refactoring</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/com/0321127420\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture</a></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":1465,"reputation":18787,"user_id":1954,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/50294c772b3985c651cc43374fe4e81d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Lee","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1954/lee"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1221939042,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1219349400,"creation_date":1219349400,"answer_id":21032,"question_id":1711,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"https://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/com/0131177052\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Working Effectively with Legacy Code</a> is a really amazing book that goes into great detail about how to properly unit test your code and what the true benefit of it is.  It really opened my eyes.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":247,"reputation":152489,"user_id":305,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":93,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/040ffc7f3327b05f01c54a8bef3ba60a?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Mark Biek","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/305/mark-biek"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1221939042,"score":5,"last_activity_date":1219349301,"creation_date":1219349301,"answer_id":21029,"question_id":1711,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"https://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/com/0201485672\" rel=\"noreferrer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Refactoring</a></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":1340,"reputation":59097,"user_id":1790,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":79,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/4awsToXL.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Jason Bunting","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1790/jason-bunting"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1221939042,"score":9,"last_activity_date":1219349197,"last_edit_date":1219349197,"creation_date":1219348705,"answer_id":20987,"question_id":1711,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"https://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/com/0131103628\" rel=\"noreferrer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">K&amp;R</a></p>\n\n<p>@Juan: I know Juan, I know - but there are some things that can only be learned by actually getting down to the task at hand. Speaking in abstract ideals all day simply makes you into an academic. It's in the application of the abstract that we truly grok the reason for their existence. :P</p>\n\n<p>@Keith: Great mention of \"The Inmates are Running the Asylum\" by Alan Cooper - an eye opener for certain, any developer that has worked with me since I read that book has heard me mention the ideas it espouses. +1</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":1392,"reputation":66242,"user_id":1862,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":59,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/2b568071163c0d009635cfa63cf6e6ac?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Kibbee","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1862/kibbee"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1221939042,"score":6,"last_activity_date":1219348948,"creation_date":1219348948,"answer_id":21006,"question_id":1711,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"https://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/com/0596528124\" rel=\"noreferrer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Mastering Regular Expressions</a></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":1733,"reputation":233,"user_id":2346,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/2329d33b6f556817498dc68c3f9d9144?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Loren Charnley","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/2346/loren-charnley"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1221939042,"score":7,"last_activity_date":1219348927,"creation_date":1219348927,"answer_id":21002,"question_id":1711,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>The Mythical Man-Month by Fred Brooks\n<a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Mythical_Man-Month\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Mythical_Man-Month</a></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":942,"reputation":1125,"user_id":1238,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6539ae86c7d8ddec66f3928beddad569?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Nate Smith","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1238/nate-smith"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1221939042,"score":6,"last_activity_date":1219348863,"last_edit_date":1219348863,"creation_date":1219348454,"answer_id":20969,"question_id":1711,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://www.pragprog.com/the-pragmatic-programmer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Pragmatic Programmer: From Journeyman to Master</a> without a doubt. The advice in it is so well presented, and simple, that it comes across as if it was 'The Common Sense Programmer'. Love it.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":1153,"reputation":3890,"user_id":1534,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/84b70f68a0fc61064fb7a03d61e2a178?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Michał Piaskowski","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1534/micha%c5%82-piaskowski"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1221939042,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1219348525,"creation_date":1219348525,"answer_id":20977,"question_id":1711,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"https://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/com/020161622X\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Pragmatic Programmer</a></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":605,"reputation":5158,"user_id":791,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0c241cebbad2e93625acc181329223cc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"bruceatk","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/791/bruceatk"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1221822673,"score":6,"last_activity_date":1218498723,"creation_date":1218498723,"answer_id":8349,"question_id":1711,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>I've been arounda while, so most books that I have found influential don't necessarily apply today.  I do believe it is universally important to understand the platform that you are developing for (both hardware and OS).  I also think it's important to learn from other peoples mistakes.  So two books I would recommend are:</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/com/0130828629\" rel=\"noreferrer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Computing Calamities</a> and <a href=\"https://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/com/1590597214\" rel=\"noreferrer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">In Search of Stupidity: Over Twenty Years of High Tech Marketing Disasters</a></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":345,"reputation":4635,"user_id":431,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":73,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/218c63deafbad65752fb5ee4a3971dea?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Mario Marinato","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/431/mario-marinato"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1221822673,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1217957304,"creation_date":1217957304,"answer_id":2602,"question_id":1711,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>Code Complete is the number one choice, but I'd also cite Gang of Four's Design Patterns and Craig Larman's Applying UML and Patterns.</p>\n\n<p>The Timeless Way of Building, by Christopher Alexander, is another great one.  Even though it's about archtecture, it's included in the bibliography of many great programming books I have already read.</p>\n\n<p>Another one, from which I'm learning lots of new things, is Data Access Patterns, by Clifton Nock.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":129,"reputation":48000,"user_id":157,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":95,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5c4d195536d28df970410dace14870c1?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"nlucaroni","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/157/nlucaroni"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1221822673,"score":7,"last_activity_date":1217899546,"creation_date":1217899546,"answer_id":1788,"question_id":1711,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>@Peter Coulton -- you don't read Knuth, you study it.</p>\n\n<p>For me, and my work... <em>Purely Functional Data Structures</em> is great for thinking and developing with functional languages in mind.</p>\n"}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":260}